+### Two hashes answer two different questions
+
+The gate digest covers the files that carry the data *and its descriptor* - typically the split
+CSVs plus `meta.json` and `quality.json`. A package digest covers the prose: README, licence,
+release notes. Keep them named separately, because they move independently:
+
+- correcting a sentence changes the package digest and **does not requalify the rows** - never report
+  a documentation edit as "bytes re-verified";
+- editing the descriptor (`quality.json`, `meta.json`) **does** change the artifact digest, because
+  the gate hashes it - that is a real requalification and needs a new artifact version, even though
+  not one row moved.
+
+When a manifest is regenerated after publication, the artifact digest is unchanged only if neither
+of those descriptor files was touched; say which digest moved rather than whether "the dataset
+changed".
+
+### A declaration of timing is not evidence of timing
+
+A gate can only check the lag the descriptor declares; it cannot know the publisher's calendar. So
+the declaration must be built from **observed release dates**, not from an assumed schedule:
+
+- record several `period_end -> published` pairs and keep the maximum, not the mean and not a round
+  number that "looks about right";
+- express the instant as a bound above every observation ("at least N days"), and say which
+  observation set it;
+- when a later release exceeds the bound, the descriptor is wrong and the timing claim must be
+  re-derived, not patched with a note.
+
+An audit that recomputes the claim from the publisher's own record is the only check that catches
+this class; a passing gate is not evidence that the numbers were available when claimed.
+
